  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nClear skies prevail across the region this morning, with only a\nsubtle breeze delaying the drop in temps at this time. However\nthat is expected to continue to diminish thru sunrise and temps\nshould tumble into the 40s for much of the area. The primary\nconcern will be the North Country valleys where widespread 30s\nare anticipated. A Frost Advisory remains in effect thru\nmorning. Elsewhere it would not surprise me to see a brief upper\n30s reading in the typically cool locations like SFM. With a\ncool start and subsidence inversion aloft, that will also make\nit difficult to get too warm this afternoon. Despite full\nsunshine it may be hard to escape the 60s today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMuch of the rest of the week will feature a fast jet to our\nnorth with a strong upper ridge centered over the southeastern\nCONUS to our south. That will be sufficient enough to prevent\nany significant warmth from entering the region, but will also\nlead to a frontal boundary at the very least draped near the\nforecast area. This is what guidance continues to key on for the\nnext chance of rain. A cold front is forecast to wash out in the\nvicinity Wed and then an area of rain may track along that into\nour backyard for Thu. Given the uncertainty and lack of strong\nforcing along the boundary, I am okay with the NBM PoP around \n50 percent for the local area.\n\nThe stronger signal is with the next wave that approaches over \nthe weekend. The upper ridge over the Southeast is forecast to\nretrograde from the late week thru the weekend. As it does so\nand weakens, an opening upper low will eject from the northern\nRockies and head our way. Despite the fact that it is outside of\nour QPF window, there is a plenty of ensemble support for\nrainfall. NBM is already coming in with high likely PoP and that\nseems reasonable given the event is occurring at the tail end of\nthe forecast range.",
